<!-- ── Debrief (shown after completion) ── -->
<div class="lab-debrief" id="lab-debrief">
  <div class="lab-debrief-label">Debrief — SQL Injection</div>

  <h3>What happened</h3>
  <p>VaultBank's customer lookup page takes user input and concatenates it directly into a SQL query string. Because the input is not parameterized, an attacker can inject arbitrary SQL syntax — breaking out of the intended query, appending UNION clauses, and reading data from any table in the database.</p>

  <h3>The vulnerable code</h3>
  <pre>const query = "SELECT * FROM customers WHERE name LIKE '%" + input + "%'";
// input comes directly from req.query.name — no parameterization</pre>

  <h3>The fix</h3>
  <p>Use parameterized queries (prepared statements) so user input is never part of the SQL string itself. The database driver treats parameters as data, not code, making injection impossible regardless of input content.</p>
  <pre>// Parameterized query — safe from injection
const query = "SELECT * FROM customers WHERE name LIKE ?";
const param = '%' + input + '%';
db.all(query, [param], callback);

// Better yet: use an ORM like Sequelize or Prisma
// Also: apply least-privilege database accounts
// Also: use a WAF to detect common injection patterns</pre>

  <h3>Real-world impact</h3>
  <p>In a real attack, the attacker systematically dumps every table in the database — user credentials, financial records, personal information. With admin password hashes, they attempt offline cracking or use the extracted data for credential stuffing against other services.</p>
  <p><strong>Equifax (2017):</strong> Attackers exploited a SQL injection vulnerability in Apache Struts to access databases containing 143 million Americans' Social Security numbers, birth dates, and addresses. The breach cost Equifax over $1.4 billion in settlements and remains one of the largest data breaches in history.</p>

  <h3>How to find this in the wild</h3>
  <p>During a pentest, look for any endpoint that accepts user input and queries a database. Inject a single quote (<code>'</code>) and watch for SQL error messages. Use tools like <code>sqlmap</code> for automated detection. Check for blind SQL injection by comparing response times with <code>SLEEP()</code> or conditional payloads like <code>' OR 1=1--</code> versus <code>' OR 1=2--</code>.</p>

